  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Raised in Niles, Michigan, Dodge learned machining in his father’s workshop before moving to Detroit with his brother John. The pair built bicycles, opened a machine shop and won major contracts supplying transmissions to Oldsmobile and engines to Ford. Their precision manufacturing made them important Ford shareholders, but they left the arrangement to produce automobiles under their own name in 1914.\n\nHorace concentrated on engineering while John handled much of the commercial side. Dodge Brothers cars quickly became national sellers, and wartime contracts expanded the Hamtramck factory into a major industrial enterprise. Horace also pursued marine engineering, commissioning fast boats and the large steamship Delphine. He and his wife Anna were significant patrons of the Detroit Symphony Orchestra. His death in 1920 ended a short career that helped turn a machine shop into an enduring automotive name.",
    "html": "<p>Raised in Niles, Michigan, Dodge learned machining in his father’s workshop before moving to Detroit with his brother John. The pair built bicycles, opened a machine shop and won major contracts supplying transmissions to Oldsmobile and engines to Ford. Their precision manufacturing made them important Ford shareholders, but they left the arrangement to produce automobiles under their own name in 1914.</p>\r\n<p>Horace concentrated on engineering while John handled much of the commercial side. Dodge Brothers cars quickly became national sellers, and wartime contracts expanded the Hamtramck factory into a major industrial enterprise. Horace also pursued marine engineering, commissioning fast boats and the large steamship Delphine. He and his wife Anna were significant patrons of the Detroit Symphony Orchestra. His death in 1920 ended a short career that helped turn a machine shop into an enduring automotive name.</p>\r\n",
